WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:05.620
Use-after-free prevention depends on explicit ownership and lifetime discipline.

2
00:00:05.720 --> 00:00:07.320
A pointer is not enough.

3
00:00:07.420 --> 00:00:13.980
The code must know who owns the allocation, who may borrow it, who may retain it, and when the object is released.

4
00:00:14.080 --> 00:00:17.600
Those rules should be visible at function boundaries and cleanup points.

5
00:00:17.880 --> 00:00:23.080
Avoid stale aliases and ambiguous cleanup responsibilities.

6
00:00:23.180 --> 00:00:30.620
If more than one pointer can reach an allocation, the design should say which component owns it and how borrowed references become invalid.

7
00:00:30.720 --> 00:00:37.440
Setting one pointer to null can help locally, but it does not clear other aliases or replace a clear ownership model.

8
00:00:37.720 --> 00:00:42.280
Handle error paths and reallocation with predictable state transitions.

9
00:00:42.380 --> 00:00:46.140
Partial initialization should clean up only what was acquired.

10
00:00:46.240 --> 00:00:52.480
Resizing should preserve the original allocation on failure and update metadata only after success.

11
00:00:52.580 --> 00:00:58.900
Cleanup should leave objects in a defined state and prevent later code from treating released storage as live.

12
00:00:59.180 --> 00:01:09.100
Finally, use clear API contracts, code review, tests, sanitizers, static analysis, and compiler diagnostics as layered defenses.

13
00:01:09.200 --> 00:01:13.120
Tools are strongest when they support maintainable lifetime design.

14
00:01:13.220 --> 00:01:18.260
Prefer straightforward ownership rules over assumptions that are only obvious to the original author.
